###  https://everythingisasign.com/ontology/eias-ontology#Wall_Behaviour_Research_publication_to_accompany_the_work_even_buildings_sway
:Wall_Behaviour_Research_publication_to_accompany_the_work_even_buildings_sway rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                                                                        :TextualWork ,
                                                                                        :Work ;
                                                                               :hasCreator :Laude_Emanuel ;
                                                                               :publicationDate "2025-05-20"^^xsd:date ;
                                                                               <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> :Laude_Emanuel ;
                                                                               :synopsis """This research explores the gendered experience of urban space through the embodied perspective of a woman navigating the city of Rotterdam. Drawing from feminist geography, the project examines how both physical structures (benches, porches, public restrooms) and social expectations shape movement, visibility, and access in the city. What may seem like neutral urban elements are revealed to be part of a socio-physical system that privileges male presence while regulating and limiting others .

Using the dérive as a central method—expanded with choreographic tools and personal reflection—the research documents how urban navigation becomes a performance of safety. The dérive, traditionally imagined as a free and purposeless wander, is shown to be a gendered act, constrained by internalised caution and external threat. The body’s reactions become data, revealing the invisible boundaries of public space .

The city is approached not only as a site of infrastructure but as a stage of social choreography, where fear, surveillance, and exclusion shape how one moves and feels. Through embodied mapping and spatial observation, the work visualises how urban design and culture interlock to form a landscape of constraint. Rather than proposing urban fixes, this project aims to make visible the everyday limitations placed on female and marginalised bodies. It asks who the city truly serves, and how spatial justice might be reimagined when we start from lived experience. As Edward Soja notes in ‘The city and spatcial justice’ (2009), if space is socially produced, then it is open to be socially changed.""" .


